arguments.content:
Applied — the label audit is now repo-wide.

`test/workflow_selfhosted_legs_test.dart` (AC1 group) gains:

```dart
test('audit: no workflow anywhere pins the generic macOS/ARM64 labels', () {
  final workflows = Directory('.github/workflows')
      .listSync()
      .where((f) => f.path.endsWith('.yml'))
      .toList();
  for (final f in workflows) {
    final jobs = jobsOf(f.path);
    for (final e in jobs.entries) {
      final runsOn = (e.value as YamlMap)['runs-on']?.toString() ?? '';
      expect(runsOn.contains('macOS') || runsOn.contains('ARM64'), isFalse, ...);
    }
  }
});
```

Every workflow under `.github/workflows/` is globbed and every job's `runs-on` is checked — no per-job whitelist, exactly as suggested. The assert is precise today: `bench-mls.yml` is bare `self-hosted` (the Linux box, correctly label-free of macOS), the macOS pins are `[self-hosted, macos-m5]`, and GitHub-hosted `macos-latest` does not match the case-sensitive `macOS`/`ARM64` check — so a future generic pin in `daily-publish.yml`/`release-appstore.yml`/any new workflow fails CI instead of re-routing an iOS-shaped job onto Linux.

Verified: 13/13 tests in `workflow_selfhosted_legs_test.dart`, including the repo-wide audit over all 30+ workflow files.

